WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.248
This paper introduces DGAPN, the Distilled Graph
Attention Policy Network,

00:00:05.328 --> 00:00:11.641
a reinforcement learning framework for generating
drug-like molecules that bind antiviral targets.

00:00:11.721 --> 00:00:16.338
It combines spatial graph attention encoding
chemistry and 3D structure,

00:00:16.418 --> 00:00:18.686
a fragment-based attentional policy,

00:00:18.766 --> 00:00:22.274
and a curiosity bonus from random network distillation,

00:00:22.354 --> 00:00:25.210
beating prior generators on binding affinity.

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:28.489
Designing molecules that optimize a property,

00:00:28.569 --> 00:00:33.586
like binding tightly to a viral protein, is a
central goal in drug discovery,

00:00:33.666 --> 00:00:34.976
yet hard to automate.

00:00:35.056 --> 00:00:38.683
The chemical space is discrete and astronomically large,

00:00:38.763 --> 00:00:40.602
making exploration difficult,

00:00:40.682 --> 00:00:44.905
and compressing a molecule's atom-and-bond graph
into a faithful,

00:00:44.985 --> 00:00:47.950
learnable representation is itself nontrivial.

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:51.352
Prior methods leave value on the table twice.

00:00:51.432 --> 00:00:56.109
First, most graph networks encode only atom
attributes and adjacency,

00:00:56.189 --> 00:00:58.797
ignoring bond features and 3D geometry,

00:00:58.877 --> 00:01:04.312
even though shape and complementarity to the
receptor pocket make a good binder.

00:01:04.392 --> 00:01:07.828
Second, building molecules atom by atom gives long,

00:01:07.908 --> 00:01:11.551
unstable trajectories and hard-to-synthesize products.

00:01:11.631 --> 00:01:16.790
This motivates encoding spatial structure while
acting over whole fragments.

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:20.756
DGAPN makes three contributions.

00:01:20.836 --> 00:01:24.423
First, spatial Graph Attention,

00:01:24.503 --> 00:01:28.089
a GNN that self-attends over node and edge
attributes and encodes 3D structure via an inverse distance matrix.

00:01:28.169 --> 00:01:31.755
Second, the Graph Attention Policy Network,

00:01:31.835 --> 00:01:35.421
an attentional policy over a dynamic fragment
action space trained with PPO.

00:01:35.501 --> 00:01:39.088
Third,

00:01:39.168 --> 00:01:42.754
a random-network-distillation curiosity bonus
giving innovation rewards for high-affinity,

00:01:42.834 --> 00:01:46.420
synthesizable molecules.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:50.214
DGAPN casts molecule generation as a Markov
decision process.

00:01:50.294 --> 00:01:53.708
At each step,

00:01:53.788 --> 00:01:57.203
the CReM library proposes valid molecules reachable
by swapping one fragment,

00:01:57.283 --> 00:02:00.697
guaranteeing synthesizable candidates.

00:02:00.777 --> 00:02:04.191
Spatial Graph Attention embeds each candidate,

00:02:04.271 --> 00:02:07.685
combining attention over atom and bond attributes
with a spatial convolution from a sparsified

00:02:07.765 --> 00:02:11.179
inverse distance matrix,

00:02:11.259 --> 00:02:14.673
so chemistry and geometry both inform the representation.

00:02:14.753 --> 00:02:18.168
An attentional policy scores candidates and samples
the next molecule,

00:02:18.248 --> 00:02:21.662
trained with PPO.

00:02:21.742 --> 00:02:25.156
Finally,

00:02:25.236 --> 00:02:28.650
random network distillation supplies an innovation
reward whose error rewards exploring novel states.

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:35.350
The primary benchmark designs novel inhibitors
binding the NSP15 site of SARS-CoV-2.

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:39.236
Binding affinity is estimated by molecular docking,

00:02:39.316 --> 00:02:43.731
using a GPU-accelerated tool on the protein's 3D
structure,

00:02:43.811 --> 00:02:49.445
starting from a dataset of purchasable molecules
with NSP15 docking scores.

00:02:49.525 --> 00:02:55.540
The method is also tested on standard QED and
penalized LogP optimization tasks.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:01.032
On docking against NSP15, DGAPN clearly beats five
state-of-the-art baselines,

00:03:01.112 --> 00:03:06.291
reaching a best score near minus ten and a mean of
minus six point seven seven,

00:03:06.371 --> 00:03:11.617
well ahead of MolDQN, with an astronomically small
p-value under Welch's t-test.

00:03:11.697 --> 00:03:16.144
It also wins constrained optimization at every
similarity threshold,

00:03:16.224 --> 00:03:23.200
and in multi-objective mode trades slight affinity
for markedly better drug-likeness and synthesizability.

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:27.259
The authors ablate each component.

00:03:27.339 --> 00:03:31.018
In a supervised setting on NSP15,

00:03:31.097 --> 00:03:34.776
loss curves over forty runs show spatial
convolution strongly improves molecular representation learning.

00:03:34.856 --> 00:03:38.535
Comparing full DGAPN against GAPN without the
innovation reward,

00:03:38.615 --> 00:03:42.294
the curiosity bonus lifts the best docking score,

00:03:42.374 --> 00:03:46.052
though it slightly worsens synthetic accessibility.

00:03:46.132 --> 00:03:49.811
Remarkably,

00:03:49.891 --> 00:03:53.570
DGAPN even beats a greedy CReM oracle that sees
intermediate docking rewards.

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:58.233
On NSP15 docking,

00:03:58.313 --> 00:04:02.597
DGAPN reaches a best score near minus ten and a
mean of minus six point seven seven,

00:04:02.677 --> 00:04:06.960
versus roughly minus eight and minus five for the
strongest baseline.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:11.323
The gap over the second-best model is statistically
overwhelming,

00:04:11.403 --> 00:04:15.687
p around ten to the minus two hundred ninth.

00:04:15.767 --> 00:04:20.050
In multi-objective mode it yields QED zero point
seven two and synthetic accessibility two point two.

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:25.964
The takeaway: combining spatial structure with
curiosity pays off for molecular design.

00:04:26.044 --> 00:04:29.578
By encoding 3D geometry through spatial graph attention,

00:04:29.658 --> 00:04:32.741
acting over whole fragments for synthesizability,

00:04:32.821 --> 00:04:36.290
and rewarding novelty with random network distillation,

00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:41.775
DGAPN designs tighter-binding, more synthesizable
drug candidates than prior methods,

00:04:41.855 --> 00:04:46.680
with a user-defined objective that dials between
affinity and drug-likeness.
